---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { siteMeta } from '../lib/site';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description={`About ${siteMeta.name}.`}>
  <article class="page">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="identity">{siteMeta.identity}</p>
      <h1>About Me</h1>
    </header>

    <h2 id="research">Research</h2>
    <p>
      My master's thesis at the ETH Zurich Systems Group,
      <a href="https://openreview.net/forum?id=WIihTnl976">LLM-Driven GPU Kernel Optimization</a>,
      was accepted as a workshop paper at NeurIPS 2026 (ML for Systems). It studies how
      general-purpose coding agents can optimize GPU kernels over long horizons when given the
      right environment, instead of a hand-engineered search procedure.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>VibeKernel</strong>: long-running agents for GPU kernel optimization. On MLA decode
        they outperformed specialized agentic systems at equal cost and produced a kernel that
        ranked in the top 10 on NVIDIA's
        <a href="https://research.nvidia.com/benchmarks/sol-execbench/leaderboard/kernel/227/B200">SOL-ExecBench leaderboard</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Experiment memory</strong>: a version-control-inspired memory system that keeps
        agents productive across sessions, reducing best kernel latency by 23.4% over conversation
        compaction alone.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Vibe-Bench</strong>: a benchmarking approach that evaluates kernels through the
        application's interface rather than in isolation. Applied to SGLang serving (2.0% faster
        than FlashInfer) and GPU database hash joins (up to 2.9× faster than NVIDIA cuCollections).
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Before that, my semester project explored LLM agent and database co-design: how database
      redesign could improve agent interaction, latency, and query generation efficiency on
      GPU-based database backends.
    </p>

    <h2>Open source</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/pull/37975">SGLang #37975</a> (open): fixed
        stale CUDA-graph metadata in the FlashInfer MLA attention backend, which could produce
        incorrect attention outputs during decode.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/pull/4504">FlashInfer #4504</a> (open):
        guarded Trace Apply kernel substitution so MLA decode kernels are no longer routed to
        incompatible prefill calls.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Experience</h2>
    <p>
      At Schranner Negotiation Institute, I led the product development of its
      <a href="https://platform.schranner.com">
        Negotiation training platform
      </a>,
      owning the roadmap and leading a four-person engineering team while architecting and building
      the product end to end. The platform combines a React and TypeScript frontend, a NestJS and
      PostgreSQL backend, Python AI services, real-time WebRTC voice agents, automated
      LLM-based feedback, and containerized deployment on Azure.
    </p>
    <p>
      Previously, as Vice-President and Project Leader at ETH juniors, I led consulting and
      recruitment mandates for clients including Roche, Zürcher Kantonalbank, IKEA, and Axpo. The
      work spanned AI systems, data products, market strategy, and organizational growth.
    </p>
    <p>
      As a Student Partner at Founderful Campus, I scouted and supported student founders across
      ETH Zurich, connecting promising teams with Founderful's venture network and initial funding.
    </p>

    <h2>Now</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Completed my ETH MSc in Electrical Engineering and Information Technology (October 2026).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
